1: # Whole-fibre zero sites and source reduction
2: 
3: ## P3K.20. Proved: the Euler contribution of a whole-fibre zero
4: 
5: For a semistable multiplicative source fibre \(I_N\), a
6: whole-fibre zero of order \(e\) has period determinant
7: \(Ne\) and central Mumford fibre Euler number \(2Ne\),
8: for every periodic unimodular triangulation.  At an
9: \(I_s^*\) zero, \(s>0\), the smooth quadratic
10: semistable Mumford root, followed by its actual monomial
11: involution quotient and toric quotient resolutions, has
12: Euler contribution at least \(4se\).  Potentially good
13: zero sites are excluded from sphere members by P3K.12.
14: 
15: In this actual smooth-root toric construction, a sphere
16: member therefore has exactly one whole-fibre zero: a
17: simple \(I_1\) zero.  For a rational rank-one source,
18: writing its generic line point and translation as
19: \(aG\) and \(bG\), respectively, the height denominator
20: \(d=1/h(G)\) satisfies \(ab=d\) after choosing the
21: contracting generator and the sign of \(G\).
22: 
23: **Proof: the actual line-frame determinant.** On the infinite
24: Tate chain let the source period and suspension period have
25: valuation vectors
26: \[
27:                     h=(N,\alpha),\qquad g=(-c,\beta).
28: \]
29: Write the fibre-coordinate graph of the actual line extension
30: as heights \(h_j\), so \(h_{j+N}=h_j+\alpha\).
31: An arbitrary vertical line divisor changes these heights
32: integrally; it is not omitted.  The line lift has the same
33: zero order \(e\) on every component precisely when its
34: actual fibre-coordinate transition has defect
35: \[
36:                       h_j+\beta-h_{j-c}=e.             \tag{P3K.20.1}
37: \]
38: Put \(r=\gcd(N,c)\), and iterate this identity \(N/r\)
39: times.  The period relation on the left gives
40: \[
41:       -\frac c r\alpha=\frac N r(\beta-e),
42:       \qquad
43:       \det(h,g)=N\beta+c\alpha=Ne.                    \tag{P3K.20.2}
44: \]
45: This is an identity of the actual component line frames.
46: It proves cancellation of the off-diagonal valuation terms
47: without changing or normalizing the vertical line divisor.
48: 
49: A fundamental polygon of this period lattice has area
50: \(Ne\).  Every unimodular triangle has area \(1/2\),
51: so it contains exactly \(2Ne\) triangles, counted with
52: their periodic boundary identifications.  In the central
53: Mumford fibre each triangle gives one zero-dimensional
54: torus orbit.  All other orbit strata are positive-dimensional
55: complex tori and have Euler number zero.  Additivity of
56: Euler number over the finite orbit stratification gives
57: \(2Ne\).  No vertical edge or semifree property of the
58: chosen delta circle is assumed in this calculation.
59: 
60: **Proof: the starred multiplicative quotient.** The quadratic
61: semistable root of an \(I_s^*\) fibre is \(I_{2s}\),
62: and the pulled-back whole-fibre zero has order \(2e\).
63: The determinant just proved is \(4se\).  The smooth
64: root Mumford fibre therefore has \(8se\) zero-dimensional
65: torus strata and Euler number \(8se\).
66: 
67: The quadratic deck is monomial on the central strata.
68: Indeed on the source nodal identity torus it is
69: \(Z\mapsto uZ^{-1}\), with a nonzero central unit
70: \(u\).  It commutes with the scalar circle on the line,
71: so in a line frame it sends \(V\mapsto f(Z)V\).
72: The source, line and their linearization are algebraic,
73: and this central torus map is regular and invertible.
74: Thus \(f\) is a unit of \(\mathbb C[Z,Z^{-1}]\),
75: so \(f(Z)=vZ^j\).  The induced maps on preserved
76: boundary torus strata are the restrictions of this same
77: integral monomial map.  Nonpreserved strata are permuted.
78: An invariant refinement can be used for descent; every
79: subdivision increases the number of maximal cones and
80: does not lower the preceding root Euler count.
81: 
82: There is also an explicit invariant unimodular height-one
83: choice, so a noncrepant refinement is not needed to obtain
84: this model.  For the generic point \(aG\), the root Tate
85: line has \(\alpha=as\) in the Tate lift \(Z_G^a\).
86: Changing that lift by a Tate period power \(Q_T^k\)
87: is the explicit line-frame map \(V\mapsto Z^kV\),
88: so this choice retains the actual line bundle and its
89: integral marking.  Vertical component divisors change
90: the graph heights periodically and do not change this
91: quasiperiod.  The deck acts on valuation
92: coordinates by
93: \(R(x,y)=(-x,y-ax)\); this follows by applying it to
94: the source period and preserving the scalar direction.
95: The whole-zero identity is \(2\beta+abs=4e\).
96: For odd \(a\), the integral shear
97: \(y'=y-(a-1)x/2\) gives \(R(x,y')=(-x,y'-x)\).
98: The positive unit-square triangulation is invariant, since
99: its edge vectors \(u,v,u+v\) go to \(-(u+v),v,-u\).
100: For even \(a\), use \(y'=y-ax/2\).  The reflection
101: is then \((-x,y')\) and the periods are
102: \((2s,0),(-bs,2e)\).  Give the square with lower-left
103: corner \((j,k)\) its diagonal according to the parity
104: \[
105:                       j+bs\left\lfloor k/(2e)\right\rfloor.
106: \]
107: Both periods preserve this parity.  Reflection sends the
108: square to \((-j-1,k)\), changes the parity, and exchanges
109: the two diagonals.  This is the required invariant
110: unimodular periodic triangulation.
111: 
112: The canonical-form normalization also identifies the
113: quotient-resolution comparison.  In Weierstrass coordinates
114: \(q=z^2,x=z^2X,y=z^3Y\), one has exactly
115: \[
116:                   (dx/y)\wedge dq=2(dX/Y)\wedge dz.
117: \]
118: After wedging the logarithmic line form, this is the
119: nowhere-zero Kulikov form
120: \(\Omega=d\log Z\wedge d\log V\wedge dz\).
121: The source inversion and \(z\mapsto-z\) give two
122: minus signs, while the additional \(Z^j\) in the line
123: map drops out of the wedge.  Thus the deck preserves
124: \(\Omega\).  Its nontrivial stabilizers on a smooth
125: root have eigenvalues \((-1,-1,+1)\), and their quotient
126: is a transverse \(A_1\) surface singularity along a fixed
127: curve.  In a resolution chart \(v=ut,w=ut^2\) for
128: \(v^2=uw\), the invariant two-form is
129: \(\tfrac12du\wedge dt\); it is nonvanishing.  This
130: is the actual crepant quotient resolution.
131: 
132: A divisor in this resolved model cannot be contracted to
133: a smooth model carrying the same nonvanishing form: the
134: Jacobian of a birational morphism vanishes along every
135: divisor mapped into codimension at least two, contradicting
136: the equality of those forms on their common open set.
137: Changes of the height-one Mumford triangulation are already
138: covered by the period-area count for each such fan.  In
139: particular a pure base-root contraction, whose pullback
140: canonical form has a ramification zero, is not being
141: silently used as a comparison of these normalized forms.
142: 
143: On an invariant torus stratum a fixed point equation is
144: \(x^{M-I}=a^{-1}\).  Its solution is empty or a coset
145: of the kernel of an integral torus homomorphism.  That
146: kernel is a finite union of complex tori.  Its Euler
147: number is nonnegative: positive-dimensional components
148: give zero and points give one.  Euler additivity therefore
149: gives \(\chi(X'^{\iota})\ge0\) on the root central fibre.
150: The finite-action orbit count, applied stratumwise, is
151: \[
152:  \chi(X'/\langle\iota\rangle)
153:        =\frac{\chi(X')+\chi(X'^{\iota})}{2}
154:        \ge4se.                                        \tag{P3K.20.3}
155: \]
156: 
157: For completeness, the quotient-resolution correction has
158: a definite sign in the stated toric construction.  Along
159: each fixed torus stratum the normal cyclic quotient has
160: constant diagonal characters.  Resolve it by a toric
161: subdivision.  A compact exceptional fibre has finitely
162: many torus orbits; its positive-dimensional orbits have
163: Euler number zero and it has at least one zero-dimensional
164: orbit.  Its Euler number is consequently at least one.
165: Euler additivity over the base fixed torus strata shows
166: that replacing a point-normal fibre by this exceptional
167: fibre adds a nonnegative quantity.  This proves the same
168: lower bound after the actual toric quotient resolutions.
169: Flips between unimodular height-one triangulations keep
170: the triangle count unchanged.
171: 
172: **Proof: the actual invariant-twist map at a semistable zero.**
173: The logarithmic modification in the supplied construction
174: \texttt{con-log} uses an extending root automorphism and
175: an invariant lattice translation, with punctured comparison
176: \(\sigma=(\log z)/(2\pi i)\,v\).  A whole-zero line
177: lift itself is not invertible on the zero fibre.  If the
178: same invariant-translation formula is interpreted on its
179: semistable Mumford torus, its quotient can nevertheless
180: be described exactly.  Full-rank unipotent monodromy has
181: invariant lattice equal to the primitive vanishing-torus
182: lattice.  Thus its twist has no gamma component.
183: On \(q=z^m\) write its deck as
184: \[
185:  (Z,V,z)\longmapsto
186:        (\zeta_m^r Z,\zeta_m^t V,\zeta_m z).
187: \]
188: The invariant coordinates are
189: \[
190:                Z'=Z/z^r,\qquad V'=V/z^t,\qquad q=z^m.
191: \]
192: They preserve every original period \(c(q)q^\Gamma\).
193: On cocharacters the quotient lattice and its exact
194: isomorphism are
195: \[
196:  \mathbb Z^3+\mathbb Z(r,t,1)/m
197:       \longrightarrow\mathbb Z^3,
198:  \qquad (x,y,s)\longmapsto(x-rs,y-ts,ms).
199: \]
200: The two horizontal basis vectors and \((r,t,1)/m\)
201: map to the three standard basis vectors.  Conjugating
202: the root period shear with translation \(m\Gamma\)
203: by this map gives the original shear \(\Gamma\).
204: Hence a smooth Kulikov target is again a Mumford fan
205: for the original period lattice and has the same Euler
206: count.  This gives the quotient directly, including
207: cases where resolving its root first would add curves.
208: No noninvariant affine operation at a zero germ has
209: been assumed beyond these actual published maps.
210: 
211: **Proof: the global zero count and height equation.** Away from
212: zero fibres, the line-complement construction has no fixed
213: point for the scalar circle, including its finite affine
214: quotients.  Its compact fibre Euler number is zero.
215: This also follows directly from the corresponding real
216: mapping-torus description.  On the smooth part of the
217: base the fibre is a complex torus and also has Euler
218: number zero.  Stratifying the base by its finitely many
219: special values therefore expresses the total Euler number
220: as the sum of the zero-site contributions above.
221: 
222: A smooth or potentially good zero site has the elliptic
223: fixed component excluded by P3K.12.  Every semistable
224: multiplicative zero contributes the positive even integer
225: \(2Ne\).  Every starred multiplicative zero in the
226: stated quotient-resolution construction contributes at
227: least four.  Since a six-sphere has Euler number two,
228: there is exactly one contribution, with \(N=e=1\).
229: 
230: The literal whole-fibre Hom relation and the telescoping
231: height calculation of P3K.14 give
232: \(\deg E=ab\,h(G)\).  Hence \(ab=d\).
233: For the pole version of the construction replace the
234: actual suspension generator \(B\) by \(B^{-1}\).
235: Their cyclic groups are identical, and the corresponding
236: period lattices are identical with the gamma generator
237: reversed.  Thus their toric quotient is identified by the
238: identity on its common charts; the inverse lift has zeros
239: in place of poles.  Finally replacing the name of the
240: generator \(G\) by \(-G\) makes the two coefficients
241: positive without changing the represented line point or
242: translation.  This gives the stated positive factorization.
243: **End of proof.**
244: 
245: The starred-fibre bound retains the normalized canonical form
246: and its actual monomial quotient/resolution maps.  The
247: invariant-twist paragraph supplies the direct quotient map
248: for the stated logarithmic formula; it does not assume an
249: additional noninvariant zero-germ operation.  The later
250: Seifert restrictions still require the orbit map of K16;
251: the present Euler count itself applies to every periodic
252: unimodular Mumford triangulation and does not impose a
253: vertical-edge or semifree delta condition.
